---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Compare from "../components/Compare.astro";
import { neighbors } from "../lib/nav";
const { prev, next } = neighbors("projects");
---

<Base title="2. Projects & hello world — C# vs TypeScript" description="2. Projects & hello world: side-by-side C# and TypeScript examples." slug="projects">
  <div slot="header" id="top">
    <p><small><a href="/">C# vs TS</a> / 2. Projects & hello world</small></p>
    <h1>2. Projects &amp; hello world</h1>
    <p><strong>How to read:</strong> left is C#, middle is TypeScript, right is blank Mystery (yours). Narrow screens stack.</p>
  </div>

  <section id="projects">
  
  <h2>2. Projects &amp; hello world</h2>
  <h3>2a. Scaffold + run</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>// dotnet new console -n HelloCs
// Program.cs — top-level statements
Console.WriteLine("Hello, C#");

// run:   dotnet run
// build: dotnet build -c Release
// test:  dotnet test
// pack:  dotnet pack</code></pre>
      <pre><code>// HelloCs.csproj
&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt;
  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt;
    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net8.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;
    &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;
    &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>// npm init -y &amp;&amp; npm i -D typescript @types/node
// hello.ts
console.log("Hello, TS");

// run (no build):  npx tsx hello.ts
// typecheck+emit:   npx tsc &amp;&amp; node dist/hello.js
// watch:           npx tsc --watch</code></pre>
      <pre><code>// tsconfig.json
&#123;
  "compilerOptions": &#123;
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "outDir": "dist",
    "declaration": true
  &#125;,
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"]
&#125;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <h3>2b. Entry points &amp; top-level await</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>// Top-level statements allowed (Program.cs)
// + top-level await since C# 9
string body = await new HttpClient()
  .GetStringAsync("https://example.com");
Console.WriteLine(body.Length);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>// Top-level await allowed in ESM modules
const res = await fetch("https://example.com");
const body = await res.text();
console.log(body.length);</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li><code>Nullable enable</code> ≈ <code>"strict": true</code>. Turn both on for new projects.</li>
    <li><code>ImplicitUsings</code> ≈ TS auto-imports — both reduce boilerplate, both can confuse newcomers about where names come from.</li>
    <li>TS ESM imports need explicit <code>.js</code> extensions (<code>./point.js</code>) even though source is <code>.ts</code> — C# <code>using</code> needs no file extension at all.</li>
  </ul>

  </section>

  <nav class="pager" aria-label="Previous / next topic">
    <span>{prev ? <a href={`/${prev.slug}/`}>← {prev.title}</a> : <span />}</span>
    <span><a href="/">All topics</a></span>
    <span>{next ? <a href={`/${next.slug}/`}>{next.title} →</a> : <span />}</span>
  </nav>
</Base>
